WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:09.830
NARRATOR: Wait.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:10.830
Wait, wait a minute.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.910
Hold it right there.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.129
Let me ask you a question.

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:17.100
What do you see here?

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.140
If you're thinking AEI, you're right.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:27.810
This is the headquarters of the American Enterprise
Institute, but at heart, AEI is more than

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.279
just a place.

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:38.350
AEI is an idea.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:44.510
It's an idea that champions individual liberty,
advances American leadership, lifts people

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.350
to their highest, and pulls them up from their
lowest.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:54.600
It's the idea that competitive, free enterprise
is what makes this all possible.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.870
While the expressions have changed and many
different faces have made their mark, the

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.820
idea has been the same since our founding.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:06.750
VERMONT ROYSTER: AEI is a nonprofit, non-partisan,
research institution located in Washington

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:07.750
DC.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.330
NARRATOR: You see, this idea will always be
needed.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:15.350
RONALD REAGAN: I'm confident that AEI will
carry on this vital work another four decades

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.350
and beyond.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:23.250
NARRATOR: Join us in continuing a legacy that
is reaffirmed every day.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:36.060
MALE PRESENTER: Ladies and gentlemen, please
welcome AEI President Arthur C. Brooks.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.060
(Applause.)

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.910
ARTHUR BROOKS: Good evening ladies and gentlemen.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.850
Please take your seats.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.270
Good evening ladies and gentlemen.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.840
I'm Arthur Brooks, president of the American
Enterprise Institute.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:56.590
Welcome to the 2018 Annual Dinner and Irving
Kristol Award.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.463
We're here tonight with old friends and new
friends and good food in this beautiful setting,

00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:07.130
to celebrate here at the National Building
Museum a community that we have built together.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:14.340
AEI is a community of 250 scholars and staff,
and 1,600 supporters.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.940
As many of you know, we were founded in 1938.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.130
Eighty years ago this year.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.740
This is our 40th annual dinner.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.950
Amazing.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:28.930
Some of you have been to most of them.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:34.200
My first AEI annual dinner was in 2007.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.420
I was a professor at Syracuse University and
I was invited as a visiting scholar at AEI

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.700
to attend this wonderful event.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:48.200
And my wife Esther and I, we came down from
Syracuse like country mice, and we looked

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:55.520
at this crowd and we said, these are people
I only ever see on TV.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:02.160
And we were watching the speaker with binoculars,
and – which gives you an idea what table

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:11.530
I got – and Esther turned to me, and she
said, can you imagine hosting this dinner?

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.170
And I said, no chance.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.209
America is such a great country.

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:20.910
Within a year, I was president of the American
Enterprise Institute.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:24.620
You never know what life has in store for
you.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.620
This is my tenth year as host to this event,
and it's also my last.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.840
As many of you know, I announced a few months
ago that this is the last year of my presidency

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.590
of AEI.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:41.310
Over the past 10 years, I've given over a
thousand public speeches in service of our

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:47.400
mission, traveling all over this great country
talking about dignity and human potential,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:53.690
and gathering support for the magnificent
work of AEI scholars, who are, just like you,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:58.420
dedicated to the principles of limited government
in a free society and the belief that America's

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.290
values are a gift to the world.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.640
What a privilege has been.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.650
When I first began this job 10 years ago – I
will be honest – I was extremely nervous

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:09.880
about speaking in public.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.620
I hadn't done it that much.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.810
It was something I needed to get better at,
and somebody gave me a piece of advice.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.340
They told me to read a book.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:25.499
Not a lofty piece of literature that our scholars
produced, but rather a book on self-improvement.

00:04:25.499 --> 00:04:32.259
It's a famous book by Dale Carnegie: “How
to Win Friends and Influence People.”

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:38.550
And in that book, Dale Carnegie brilliantly
travels all over the United States and asks

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.759
the most successful people the secrets to
their success.

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:49.120
In that book, when he's talking to a great
magician of the time in the 1920s, Howard

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.419
Thurston he asks “what is the secret to
your performance, you're the greatest performer

00:04:54.419 --> 00:05:02.229
of your generation,” and Howard Thurston,
the magician he said “simple, gratitude.”

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:07.460
He said, “every night before I go out on
stage I recite this little meditation.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.460
I am grateful because these people came to
see me.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.370
They make it possible for me to make my living
in a very agreeable way.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:20.189
I'm going to give them the very best I possibly
can,” and then, right before we stepped

00:05:20.189 --> 00:05:22.540
on the stage he said “I love my audience.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.139
I love my audience.”

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:33.150
That gave me a lot of help early on in this
in this job because gratitude and practice

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.919
really are the secret to good performance.

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:39.449
But I was thinking about that earlier today
and it occurred to me that as I step on stage

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:44.809
tonight and I gave that same little meditation
of gratitude and love, it's not just to calm

00:05:44.809 --> 00:05:49.380
my nerves, it's really a sincere expression
of the gratitude, and I love – and love

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.300
that I feel for the people in this room.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:56.150
And I want to take just a couple of minutes
and tell you why I have so much gratitude

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:57.300
toward you.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:05.689
To begin with, here tonight among this group
are my true intellectual heroes and some of

00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:08.360
my closest friends.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:15.460
As a graduate student many years ago, I remember
reading the works of great AEI scholars Leon

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:20.759
Kass and Nick Eberstadt and Michael Novak.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:24.270
And I thought to myself, I will never meet
these people.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:31.050
But they helped me to understand that my views
about individual liberty and American strength,

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.180
they're not weird.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:39.830
And I can be an intelligent, cultured, sophisticated
person, and maybe even a public or professional

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:43.330
intellectual and hold these views.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:46.330
I'm so grateful for that.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:52.029
Nothing gives me greater satisfaction than
to support the work of the scholars and staff

00:06:52.029 --> 00:06:53.050
of AEI.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:58.099
And in particular I want to mention one.

00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:03.080
I want to mention the scholar that has had
more influence on my thinking than any other

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:10.949
single individual, and that's Charles Murray.

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:15.870
(Applause)

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.490
Charles Murray retired from full-time service
at AEI this year.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.929
You wouldn't know it based on his productivity.

00:07:22.929 --> 00:07:26.449
You can't tell when Charles Murray's retired.

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:31.050
Charles Murray is somebody that I started
reading many, many years ago.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:37.669
And when I read his work, I thought, if I
could use data and social science like that

00:07:37.669 --> 00:07:44.219
to try to express things that are going on
in human life, then I would be successful.

00:07:44.219 --> 00:07:50.539
And when I finally came to a AEI, and I was
president of AEI, and I called up Charles,

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:52.580
and I said, Charles can we have lunch?

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:58.639
And he said, “you're the president of AEI.”

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:03.740
And we got together, and I realized that he's
not just a great scholar, he's a great friend.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:08.669
He was going to be, and indeed he has become
a role model for me in so many ways.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:13.719
What a gift it's been to be a small part of
his extraordinary career.

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:18.869
My life has also been immeasurably enriched
by my friendships with people in this room,

00:08:18.869 --> 00:08:24.599
people like Tony Friedman and Dan D’Aniello,
the (current ?) chairman of AEI’s extraordinary

00:08:24.599 --> 00:08:28.680
board, and so many other members of this community.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:33.610
These people have not only supported me as
I've been the head of this organization, they

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:35.330
patiently taught me about leadership.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:40.180
If things have gone well at AEI, it's because
of them.

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:45.790
The second source of my gratitude was probably
even more obvious than that.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:53.370
As you know – as you should know, this institution
doesn't just run on good feelings, although

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:54.370
we have them.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:09:01.710
We spend about $50 million a year to build
our idea agenda and communicate it to millions

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.340
of people every year around the world.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:09.910
We actually have the most inefficient business
model in America: we're in the business of

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:17.470
losing $50 million a year in intellectual
property, and you make that possible.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.090
It's incredible.

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:22.870
We combine your financial resources with the
intellectual resources of our scholars, and

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:27.210
staff to produce the expression of our shared
values, on behalf of people at the periphery

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.470
of society.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:34.540
But here's the greatest source of my gratitude,
as I finish tonight.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:41.000
At AEI, we have a subversive mission that
you support.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.840
From the earliest days, AEI’s model was
that a competition of ideas is fundamental

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.340
to a free society.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.520
Think about that.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.120
A competition of ideas.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:54.070
Not agreement on one thing, but to compete
freely.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.070
Why?

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:58.130
Because competition brings excellence.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:01.740
Monopoly brings stagnation, and it brings
mediocrity.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:08.680
That's true in the economy and it's true in
electoral politics and it's true in ideas.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:15.150
To get true excellence we need to disagree,
and disagree freely with civility, and respect

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.180
and even love for each other.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.890
That's what we stand for.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:22.280
No party line.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.830
(Applause.)

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:34.580
We have a crisis in this country today of
too many people in public life not dedicated

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:40.400
enough to the competition of ideas, but rather
too many people intent on shutting down that

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:47.100
competition with insults and intimidation
and even contempt.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:52.700
That leads to mediocrity, but it also is morally
disordered.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:11:00.430
Every single person in this room loves someone
with whom they disagree politically.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:05.810
In our culture of contempt, when strangers
on your side politically urge you to say that

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:10.870
your loved ones are stupid and evil, we have
a major problem.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:17.830
I am most grateful to be a member of a community
that rejects the culture of contempt.

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:22.640
We are countercultural, and that's fine.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.150
We simply do our work and we remember our
mission principles.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:33.490
Let me remind you of these mission principles:
that we are brothers and sisters; human dignity

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:41.280
is radically equal for all of us in all circumstances
in all stages of life, every place in the

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.000
world.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:50.730
(Applause.)

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:55.220
That progress, progress – were progressives.

00:11:55.220 --> 00:12:01.420
Progress is always possible by unleashing
the greatest source of energy in history,

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:06.730
which is human potential, and it is the American
free enterprise system that has pulled billions

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:11.590
of our brothers and sisters out of poverty
in our lifetimes, that is the most potent

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:17.820
system ever discovered to instantiate the
human potential.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.320
(Applause)

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:30.770
And it’s America's international leadership
– economic, cultural, diplomatic and military

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:37.150
– that has set people free around the world
to live up to their highest potential.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:44.170
And with our hard work and with our prayers,
America will always do so.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.280
(Applause.)

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:54.180
Let me sum up what we're all about at AEI.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:13:01.390
We want America to live up to its promise
as a country of ambitious riff-raff pursuing

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:09.070
happiness, building something good and meaningful,
earning our success, sharing our values, and

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:11.200
serving each other.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.810
These are the principles that are written
in the hearts of the people in this room,

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:23.030
and instantiated every day by the scholars
and staff, supporters, trustees, and friends

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.810
of the American Enterprise Institute.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:32.920
What fills me with gratitude the most is having
been given the gift of leading not this organization,

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:36.220
but indeed this movement for the past 10 years.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:42.960
God bless you, and thank you so very much.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.520
(Applause.)

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:59.720
Thank you and now it's on to our program,
and a wonderful program it's going to be.

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:05.540
The intellectual program comes first, then
supper, and then the serious business of drinking

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.960
and exchanging business cards.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:09.280
(Laughter.)

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:13.540
Tonight we're honored as always at this event,
to the bestow of AEI’s annual award, the

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:17.570
Irving Kristol Award, named in memory of our
beloved friend and colleague, the late Irving

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:19.560
Kristol.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:24.779
Before we introduce this year's winner, I
want to turn the microphone over to Bill Kristol,

00:14:24.779 --> 00:14:28.490
friend of so many of us for so many years,
the son of Irving Kristol and editor-at-large

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:29.490
of the Weekly Standard.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:34.130
He's going to speak for a moment about his
father and his legacy.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:38.529
Ladies and gentlemen, Bill Kristol (Applause)

00:14:38.529 --> 00:14:46.070
BILL KRISTOL: Good evening.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:51.730
It's an honor to join Arthur in welcoming
you to this annual dinner and congratulating

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:56.400
Boris Johnson on the – I think it's the
15th Irving Kristol Award.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.430
It's a distinguished group that Boris joins.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:04.500
Before that, actually, there were about a
quarter century of Francis Boyer Awards, so

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:10.920
that's also a very distinguished group if
you look it up going back to 1977.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:16.041
I remember when Chris DeMuth in 2003 told
my father that the AEI board had voted to

00:15:16.041 --> 00:15:21.560
change the annual award and lecture from the
Francis Boyer lecture to the Irving Kristol

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:27.490
lecture, he was – my father was not much
moved by these kinds of honors, honestly,

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:30.072
but with that one meant something to him.

00:15:30.072 --> 00:15:31.390
AEI meant so much to him.

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:35.400
And I remember later on talking to him, and
saying – but don't you feel a little guilty,

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:37.990
a little bad about Francis Boyer?

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:42.910
And my father said, “No, not really.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:44.860
He had a good run.”

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:50.180
You know, I think the Irving Kristol award
has had a good run and hopefully has a few

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:54.850
more years at it, but it's a dog-eat-dog world
out there, you know.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:16:01.840
Creative destruction, all that stuff that
people at AEI are in favor of.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:04.460
So anyway, it has been a wonderful group.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:10.130
I mean, preceding Boris, people like Charles
Murray, whom Arthur mentioned, and Rabbi Jonathan

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:12.120
Sacks, and Benjamin Netanyahu, Leon Kass.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:19.580
And looking over the list I was reminded that
Charles Krauthammer who sadly, as you know,

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:26.730
died this summer, was the second awardee and
really a wonderful talk worth rereading, that

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.190
he gave.

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:33.980
My mother, who was slightly indisposed -- she's
in good shape, but couldn't be here tonight

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:39.680
– sends her best to so many old friends
and former colleagues.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:40.930
She congratulates Boris.

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:45.990
She was pleased, so she told me -- it's good
that they're giving the award to a magazine

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:47.540
editor.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:51.970
And I said – I mean, my mother you know
has read – she said to me, I read the London's

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:58.980
-- I read The Spectator before Boris Johnson
was born, back in the 40s and 50s, and read

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:03.920
it under his editorship in the, I guess, late
1990s, early part of the 2000s, and has read

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:09.490
it since, and -- but I said you, know mom,
I mean Boris became Mayor of London for two

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:15.019
terms I think it was, and then Foreign Secretary,
he's not just a magazine editor really.

00:17:15.019 --> 00:17:20.500
And my mother said, for the Kristol’s, magazine
editor is the highest title.

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:28.499
Which I of course, certainly agree with.

00:17:28.499 --> 00:17:32.620
I really just want to say a word, but not
about my father at this time, but about Arthur.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:37.460
And Arthur didn't know I was going to do this,
but his 10 years really have been extraordinary

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.160
here at AEI.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:43.850
He followed Chris DeMuth, who's I think it
was 22 years, were also extraordinary.

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:53.759
And the lesson I take in management and institutions
from the tenure of Chris and Arthur is that

00:17:53.759 --> 00:17:58.289
it's really a good thing for an institution
to have excellent leaders.

00:17:58.289 --> 00:17:59.289
(Laughter.)

00:17:59.289 --> 00:18:03.190
I recommend it to everyone else here who's
running institutions.

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:05.789
I mean, it really is – it's a high standard
obviously.

00:18:05.789 --> 00:18:10.909
I think of that a third of a century, under
Chris and Arthur being the preeminent think-tank

00:18:10.909 --> 00:18:15.210
I think in Washington and in the country,
perhaps in the world – a place that really

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:21.610
respects excellence, but also respects diversity
of views and approaches, that really shows

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:24.250
that excellence and diversity can really go
together.

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:28.890
A place that has always resisted, I think
what's fashionable, tried to look beyond the

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:37.059
headlines while also influencing the headlines
at times; tried to follow intellectual conclusions

00:18:37.059 --> 00:18:40.899
to their end, and not worry too much about
whether they're popular, at least in the short

00:18:40.899 --> 00:18:41.899
term.

00:18:41.899 --> 00:18:47.490
I mean all those things were really an achievement
of actually the predecessors to Chris and

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:52.179
Arthur, but especially of Chris and Arthur,
and especially in this last decade of Arthur.

00:18:52.179 --> 00:18:57.320
He took over an institution that was already
strong and made it stronger yet.

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:03.970
It's a very good example I think for us in
this day and age that shunning vulgarity,

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:09.409
resisting conformity, avoiding mere popularity
is the right thing to do.

00:19:09.409 --> 00:19:15.460
It's very much in been in the long-term interest
of a -- which stands as an example now, I

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:19.749
think – not just other think tanks, but
actually to the academic world.

00:19:19.749 --> 00:19:24.360
Arthur's stepping down as you know as president
of AEI, and is also stepping down by going

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:25.480
to Harvard.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:31.399
I hope – I mean I say this, I shouldn't
I say this – I say this as a loyal Harvard

00:19:31.399 --> 00:19:37.750
man and -- but it's the true fact, honestly,
that the intellectual, the caliber of the

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:43.269
work of AEI the commitment to truth, the commitment
to honest and respectful discourse, but also

00:19:43.269 --> 00:19:48.559
serious discourse in terms of calling people
to account, is I think something that Arthur

00:19:48.559 --> 00:19:56.519
can bring to Harvard from AEI or -- good luck
with that, Arthur.

00:19:56.519 --> 00:20:02.659
But I do want to say that I really think AEI
is an example not just for DC, but for the

00:20:02.659 --> 00:20:04.360
country, and indeed for the world.

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:07.059
An example we need more than ever.

00:20:07.059 --> 00:20:11.999
Maybe I could even ask you – I don't have
a glass here, but to raise a glass and join

00:20:11.999 --> 00:20:16.600
me and in toasting Arthur Brooks at this -- what
will be I suppose the last annual dinner he'll

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:17.600
preside over.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:21.909
He will of course -- there'll be a huge number
I'm sure of events before he leaves in the

00:20:21.909 --> 00:20:28.230
spring and summer Arthur's planning a whole
bunch of them, I believe – (laughter) – commemorating

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:29.230
his tenure.

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:35.769
But since this is the last annual dinner he'll
be at, let's offer a toast to our friend and

00:20:35.769 --> 00:20:38.759
our leader, Arthur Brooks.

00:20:38.759 --> 00:20:49.559
ARTHUR BROOKS: Thank you, Bill, and thank
you to all of you.

00:20:49.559 --> 00:20:55.009
One of the great -- one of the many great
things about this job is being able to meet

00:20:55.009 --> 00:20:57.270
the most interesting people in the world.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.700
And it's actually easy.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.549
You call them up and say, would you like to
come to a AEI?

00:21:02.549 --> 00:21:05.149
And they almost always say yes.

00:21:05.149 --> 00:21:09.159
It's incredible, and tonight's award winner
is one of those people.

00:21:09.159 --> 00:21:12.470
Somebody I've always wanted to meet.

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:15.210
Probably you have always wanted to meet him
too.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.739
He is a member of the British Parliament.

00:21:18.739 --> 00:21:22.940
He was the UK Secretary of State for Foreign
and Commonwealth Affairs.

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:25.830
He was the Mayor of London.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:30.269
He's a best-selling author, and renowned historian.

00:21:30.269 --> 00:21:36.980
He's a cult figure in the UK, as a matter
of fact, famous for everything, from tackling

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:44.460
a German politician in a soccer match, to
having a bicycle named after him, to his hair.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:48.470
And I say that just because I'm jealous.

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:55.020
Most importantly this is a man who loves liberty,
who values free enterprise, and who understands

00:21:55.020 --> 00:22:00.239
the role that his country and our country
play in the future of the world.

00:22:00.239 --> 00:22:05.330
Ladies and gentlemen, on behalf of the entire
AEI community, our scholars, staff and trustees,

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:10.879
it is my honor to officially award the American
Enterprise Institute’s 2018 Irving Kristol

00:22:10.879 --> 00:22:13.380
Award to the Honorable Boris Johnson.

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:14.380
(Applause)

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:18.980
BORIS JOHNSON: Thank you very much.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.980
Thank you very much, Arthur.

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:26.179
Sit down – Thank you.

00:22:26.179 --> 00:22:28.580
I’m most honored.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.179
Thank you.

00:22:30.179 --> 00:22:31.780
Come on.

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:33.380
Sit down.

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:37.380
And we’ll get through dinner.

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:38.980
Thank you.

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:41.380
I’m very honored.

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:44.580
ARTHUR BROOKS: Hello, Boris.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.059
BORIS JOHNSON: Good evening, Arthur.

00:22:46.059 --> 00:22:48.100
ARTHUR BROOKS: How wonderful to see you here.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:53.369
How delighted I was the -- I was under stress
because you had votes last night in the Parliament

00:22:53.369 --> 00:22:57.590
and you had to come this morning, and we were
watching your flight all the way across the

00:22:57.590 --> 00:22:58.590
Atlantic.

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:02.679
BORIS JOHNSON: Well, wild horses wouldn't
have kept me away, and I'm absolutely thrilled

00:23:02.679 --> 00:23:05.869
to be here at the American Enterprise Institute.

00:23:05.869 --> 00:23:09.320
I take -- I was -- you could have knocked
me down with a feather when I got the news

00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:14.512
that you were going to give me the Irving
Kristol Award, and I haven't been so surprised

00:23:14.512 --> 00:23:22.159
since I was 25 and I got a letter from Monash
University, Melbourne saying that I was going

00:23:22.159 --> 00:23:26.340
to be a professor of European thought.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:33.220
It turned out to be a case of mistaken identity.

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:34.220
(Laughter.)

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:37.630
Sincerely, that’s not the case tonight,
but it was – this is a very important institution.

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:46.190
I followed the work of AEI scholars and of
people associated with this body, for many,

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:55.450
many years, and you have been incredibly influential,
and drivers for change and for things I believe

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:56.450
in.

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:57.840
And it's great to be here tonight.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:58.840
ARTHUR BROOKS: Thank you Boris.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:01.009
Thank you for your generosity on behalf of
all of us.

00:24:01.009 --> 00:24:06.269
I want to start -- we're going to talk about
highfalutin things this evening, but I want

00:24:06.269 --> 00:24:08.739
to start at the personal level.

00:24:08.739 --> 00:24:11.169
Something that a lot of people here don't
know about you.

00:24:11.169 --> 00:24:12.169
You were born in America.

00:24:12.169 --> 00:24:15.249
BORIS JOHNSON: I was.

00:24:15.249 --> 00:24:17.639
In New York, where my parents were.

00:24:17.639 --> 00:24:24.179
I wanted to be close to my mother at the time,
as they say, and I – it was a very expensive

00:24:24.179 --> 00:24:30.470
decision, because it turned out that the United
States – what you call it, the Inland Revenue?

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:31.470
ARTHUR BROOKS: The Internal Revenue Service.

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:32.970
BORIS JOHNSON: The Internal Revenue Service
–

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:35.940
ARTHUR BROOKS: Well acquainted to the people
in this room.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:37.309
BORIS JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:24:37.309 --> 00:24:38.309
Well, anyway.

00:24:38.309 --> 00:24:45.230
In order to help you guys out, they pursued
me for the tax on the sale of my primary dwelling

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:52.440
in London, even though I hadn't lived in America
for 45 years, which I thought was excessive.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:53.440
(Laughter.)

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:57.500
And so with great reluctance, and it was -- it
was a very emotional moment.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:01.230
With great reluctance I had to forswear that
citizenship.

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:02.651
ARTHUR BROOKS: So you had to renounce your
citizenship.

00:25:02.651 --> 00:25:03.659
BORIS JOHNSON: What could I do?

00:25:03.659 --> 00:25:10.509
I’m like the man in the Bible, I went soaring
-- for he had great possessions.

00:25:10.509 --> 00:25:12.350
Not great possessions in my case, but enough.

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:16.659
ARTHUR BROOKS: So you realize that this makes
it impossible for you to be elected president

00:25:16.659 --> 00:25:17.690
of the United States?

00:25:17.690 --> 00:25:20.779
BORIS JOHNSON: Yes.

00:25:20.779 --> 00:25:21.809
(Laughter.)

00:25:21.809 --> 00:25:24.840
ARTHUR BROOKS: Tell me about your upbringing.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:28.279
One of the things that we have in common,
is that both of our mothers were professional

00:25:28.279 --> 00:25:29.279
painters.

00:25:29.279 --> 00:25:31.359
I found that interesting, and so tell me about
your childhood.

00:25:31.359 --> 00:25:32.740
Tell me about your upbringing a little bit.

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:34.250
BORIS JOHNSON: Well it's absolutely true.

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:42.159
My mother -- my mother's is -- she is a painter,
and I've always wanted to paint very badly

00:25:42.159 --> 00:25:49.389
myself, and indeed do paint very badly – I
do – but I – you know, it's a -- she's

00:25:49.389 --> 00:25:50.929
been an inspiration to me.

00:25:50.929 --> 00:26:00.440
She's by the way, she's very much on the Left,
and -- I don't what the relevance of that

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:01.650
is, but she is.

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:04.750
But she -- I'm thoroughly supportive of painters
everywhere.

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:05.750
(Laughter.)

00:26:05.750 --> 00:26:11.269
ARTHUR BROOKS: Well, it’s good to know,
that Boris has the painter vote.

00:26:11.269 --> 00:26:15.679
And you were you're brought up in a mixed
household, left and right.

00:26:15.679 --> 00:26:18.330
What brought you to your politics that you
currently have?

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:23.519
BORIS JOHNSON: Funnily enough I was pretty
-- I was pretty kind of middle-of-the-road

00:26:23.519 --> 00:26:31.980
until university, where I was the exposure
to other left-wing students – bourgeois,

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:35.840
affluent, hypocritical left-wing students
–

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:40.340
ARTHUR BROOKS: Ah, the best kind.

00:26:40.340 --> 00:26:46.190
BORIS JOHNSON: -- and their virulent hatred
of Margaret Thatcher, about whom I had no

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:49.489
really strong views until then.

00:26:49.489 --> 00:26:56.769
And they supported the miners’ leader, Arthur
Scargill, in his attempt to bring down the

00:26:56.769 --> 00:27:00.710
democratically elected government of the UK.

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:06.059
I first felt the stirrings of conservative
sentiment then.

00:27:06.059 --> 00:27:09.159
ARTHUR BROOKS: Fundamentally, you are a contrarian.

00:27:09.159 --> 00:27:11.859
BORIS JOHNSON: Well, I suppose I was.

00:27:11.859 --> 00:27:13.320
That's a good insight, Arthur.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:21.750
Yes, I was a – (laughter) – my right wing
feelings were triggered, to use the modern

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:27.000
word, by a sense of outrage at their gluttonous
hypocrisy.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:30.529
ARTHUR BROOKS: So why don't we call it that
in the United States?

00:27:30.529 --> 00:27:32.730
The gluttonous hypocrisy.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:34.629
Would that win elections in America?

00:27:34.629 --> 00:27:35.861
BORIS JOHNSON: I don’t know.

00:27:35.861 --> 00:27:38.549
I think you should give it a go.

00:27:38.549 --> 00:27:44.679
ARTHUR BROOKS: So when was your first elective
office?

00:27:44.679 --> 00:27:53.710
BORIS JOHNSON: As a student, I won something
as a student I think, but my first elected

00:27:53.710 --> 00:28:03.489
-- I tried to get elected in Wales in 1997,
which was the year of the biggest conservative

00:28:03.489 --> 00:28:10.450
route since 1906, and I fought -- I fought
Clwyd South and Clwyd South fought back in

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:18.360
a big way, and I didn't win then, but I then
got in in 2001 in Henley.

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:21.279
ARTHUR BROOKS: And you've been in public life
ever since?

00:28:21.279 --> 00:28:22.279
Almost.

00:28:22.279 --> 00:28:23.279
BORIS JOHNSON: Yes.

00:28:23.279 --> 00:28:24.529
I was mayor of London a couple of times.

00:28:24.529 --> 00:28:26.940
ARTHUR BROOKS: I've heard that.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:31.629
The -- one of the things that you're really
well known for, besides public life, besides

00:28:31.629 --> 00:28:37.499
political life is being a best-selling author,
which is pretty unusual.

00:28:37.499 --> 00:28:42.250
It's particularly a highbrow intellectual
work, which your “Churchill Factor” most

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:43.250
certainly is.

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:47.299
Churchill is somebody who admire a great deal,
and in that great book -- which I recommend

00:28:47.299 --> 00:28:53.980
all of you for your interest -- Boris writes,
“Churchill matters today, because he saved

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:55.870
our civilization.

00:28:55.870 --> 00:29:00.559
And the important point is that only he could
have done it.”

00:29:00.559 --> 00:29:05.409
Tell me a little bit about your admiration
for Winston Churchill, and the key lessons

00:29:05.409 --> 00:29:08.409
that you learned from him today, that we can
all learn from him today.

00:29:08.409 --> 00:29:15.269
BORIS JOHNSON: Well the key thing about Churchill
was that he was so fantastically brave, you

00:29:15.269 --> 00:29:16.269
know.

00:29:16.269 --> 00:29:21.330
He wasn't a big guy, and he was – he was
bullied at school, he was pelted with cricket

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:22.330
balls, and he ran away.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:27.710
He was only five foot six and half inches
tall, and he had a thirty-inch chest as a

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:28.710
young man.

00:29:28.710 --> 00:29:30.909
He was already -- he was a runty shrimpy kind
of guy.

00:29:30.909 --> 00:29:32.220
You didn't know that.

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:38.909
But he turned himself by sheer effort of will
into this extraordinary colossus who dominated

00:29:38.909 --> 00:29:43.799
so much of British politics and world politics
in the 20th century.

00:29:43.799 --> 00:29:51.279
And the key point for me is that although
he made so many mistakes and got so many things

00:29:51.279 --> 00:29:59.159
wrong, from Gallipoli to the abdication crisis
to going back on gold to his attitude towards

00:29:59.159 --> 00:30:00.159
Indian independence.

00:30:00.159 --> 00:30:02.409
He got things catastrophically wrong.

00:30:02.409 --> 00:30:13.419
But the one big thing he got right in May
1940, when the UK had to make a decision about

00:30:13.419 --> 00:30:21.450
whether to fight on or to do a deal with Mussolini
and with Hitler.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:27.649
And if Churchill hadn't been in that room,
there's no doubt at all in my mind that we

00:30:27.649 --> 00:30:31.080
would have come to terms of some kind.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:36.509
And he made all the difference because he
basically decided that it would be a disaster

00:30:36.509 --> 00:30:42.059
for his country, for the British Empire, and
for civilization if we did that deal.

00:30:42.059 --> 00:30:49.270
And within a year of that decision 30,000
British men, women, and children had been

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:52.440
killed by Nazi bombs.

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:56.470
Thirty thousand.

00:30:56.470 --> 00:31:04.489
But as a result of his decision to fight on,
I believe that he saved the European continent

00:31:04.489 --> 00:31:06.529
from absolute barbarism.

00:31:06.529 --> 00:31:07.529
(Applause.)

00:31:07.529 --> 00:31:21.470
And so it was -- it was a willingness – I’ll
just look at this --

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:28.510
it was a willingness to think for himself
and to be brave that we need to -- we need

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:29.860
to follow that today.

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:32.299
ARTHUR BROOKS: Give me an example.

00:31:32.299 --> 00:31:38.419
Give me an example of something where we need
to be brave, to follow our instincts to more

00:31:38.419 --> 00:31:39.450
noble ends.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:45.850
BORIS JOHNSON: Well, since you since you drag
it from me kicking and screaming.

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:48.039
Actually you didn't, but I'll tell you anyway.

00:31:48.039 --> 00:31:55.130
I think that we need -- when you look at our
country, when you look at the UK -- we have

00:31:55.130 --> 00:32:01.629
a great choice right now, and we are in the
throes of -- some of you may have seen from

00:32:01.629 --> 00:32:06.730
the news -- we're in the throes of deciding
exactly how to carry out this Brexit.

00:32:06.730 --> 00:32:07.730
We did quite well.

00:32:07.730 --> 00:32:09.600
We got through almost ten minutes without
Brexit.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.379
ARTHUR BROOKS: Well, it's all over now.

00:32:12.379 --> 00:32:14.440
BORIS JOHNSON: But we have a choice.

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:19.460
And I would just say to all you scholars,
and supporters of the American Enterprise

00:32:19.460 --> 00:32:25.570
Institute, this is a critical moment for everybody
who cares about free markets, about competition,

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:30.320
about global free trade, about all the things
-- as you were saying earlier, Arthur -- all

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:33.169
things that lifted billions of people out
of poverty.

00:32:33.169 --> 00:32:40.979
The UK could be about to come loose of the
European system of regulation and government.

00:32:40.979 --> 00:32:47.159
It could be about to enter the global system
as an independent economic actor again, able

00:32:47.159 --> 00:32:51.909
to campaign for pro-competitive policies.

00:32:51.909 --> 00:32:54.129
(Applause.)

00:32:54.129 --> 00:33:03.760
That's the opportunity, and I hope very much
that, with you with you guys, with America,

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:05.499
we can work together to deliver that agenda.

00:33:05.499 --> 00:33:09.509
And you know there's a conversation going
on in my country about exactly how to do it.

00:33:09.509 --> 00:33:10.509
It is difficult.

00:33:10.509 --> 00:33:18.059
But the last thing we want now, is for us
the Brits, having made this momentous decision

00:33:18.059 --> 00:33:24.179
to go for Brexit, to be sucked back by the
tractor beam of Brussels.

00:33:24.179 --> 00:33:30.730
The tractor beam is the word I want -- into
the orbit, the regulatory lunar pool –

00:33:30.730 --> 00:33:34.889
ARTHUR BROOKS: Brussels is the Death Star,
in case you are not following the reference

00:33:34.889 --> 00:33:35.889
–

00:33:35.889 --> 00:33:36.889
BORIS JOHNSON: No.

00:33:36.889 --> 00:33:37.889
I lived in Brussels.

00:33:37.889 --> 00:33:38.889
My daughter was born in Brussels.

00:33:38.889 --> 00:33:39.909
ARTHUR BROOKS: Some of his best friend are
in Brussels.

00:33:39.909 --> 00:33:42.320
BORIS JOHNSON: I love Brussels.

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:44.489
It’s a wonderful town.

00:33:44.489 --> 00:33:45.489
I love Belgium.

00:33:45.489 --> 00:33:46.940
But the fact is that –

00:33:46.940 --> 00:33:50.090
ARTHUR BROOKS: Beer, chocolate, bureaucracy.

00:33:50.090 --> 00:33:55.239
BORIS JOHNSON: The EU, for all its achievements
-- and there have been great achievements

00:33:55.239 --> 00:34:00.429
over the last 50, 60 years -- 70 years of
the of the EU's existence.

00:34:00.429 --> 00:34:06.599
For all its achievements, it really is no
longer right in its current form for the UK.

00:34:06.599 --> 00:34:08.200
We've done the right thing.

00:34:08.200 --> 00:34:09.740
We’ve done the right thing.

00:34:09.740 --> 00:34:10.740
(Applause.)

00:34:10.740 --> 00:34:17.740
But having done that -- having done that -- we've
got to make sense of it, and there is no point

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:25.740
at all in us coming out of the EU, only two
remain effectively in large measure run by

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:26.740
the EU.

00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:31.150
If you're going to take back control, then
take back control, and use it for a purpose.

00:34:31.150 --> 00:34:36.720
So I hope very much it will get the support
of you guys in achieving that objective.

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:37.720
(Applause.)

00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:43.380
ARTHUR BROOKS: I'm going to come back to this
issue in just a minute, but I want to go slightly

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:47.560
backward, to another book that you wrote that
bears on this subject.

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:52.190
He wrote a very popular book about the history
of Rome.

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:55.470
An issue on which, or a subject in which you
have some expertise.

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:59.120
And in it you observed that one of the great
things that the Romans did was to create a

00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:03.420
common identity across cultures and languages
and tribes.

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:09.810
And yet you have also said that you believe
that Rome, and the European Union bear relatively

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:10.910
little resemblance.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:16.090
So tell me -- tell me why they're so different,
such that we can get back to Brexit and your

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:20.190
understanding of your separation from the
EU, and how it's in your interest.

00:35:20.190 --> 00:35:25.650
BORIS JOHNSON: I was living in in Brussels
actually, and thinking deeply -- as I always

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:32.741
do -- about the European civilization, and
its relationship with its past, and I have

00:35:32.741 --> 00:35:40.520
no doubt at all but in a deep Freudian way,
the European integration is the (federalists

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:46.642
?) -- want to recreate that idea of this great
unified whole, that was represented by the

00:35:46.642 --> 00:35:47.642
Roman Empire.

00:35:47.642 --> 00:35:48.642
That’s at the core.

00:35:48.642 --> 00:35:52.560
You look at what some of the – Mario Prodi
said the same thing just the other day.

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:55.940
And it was an amazing thing the Roman Empire.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:36:02.110
It lasted, well, from 73 BC to wherever you
dated to, you know.

00:36:02.110 --> 00:36:04.260
Some say it still hasn't fallen, you know.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:08.340
Whatever, you know, 476 AD I’d suppose you'd
say was Roman.

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:09.800
It was a long time.

00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:20.690
It was fantastically successful, and I think
the differences between the EU, and the Roman

00:36:20.690 --> 00:36:27.240
Empire are very instructive, because what
you've got now is an attempt to create this

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:34.160
unity, in this whole with a flag, and with
anthems, and a sort of political union, and

00:36:34.160 --> 00:36:39.080
then I was talking about creating a European
president and that kind of thing.

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:47.960
But what it lacks is that single charismatic
sense of identity and allegiance that the

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.160
Roman Empire had.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:54.510
Everybody knew the Emperor, and everybody
-- actually everybody important around the

00:36:54.510 --> 00:37:02.350
Roman Empire had to swear loyalty to the Emperor,
and in some cases actually worship the Emperor.

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:10.270
And the only real comparator with the Roman
Empire today in terms of that pungent sense

00:37:10.270 --> 00:37:14.310
of identity -- a pluribus unum -- you know
where I'm going with this.

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:17.060
ARTHUR BROOKS: I have an idea, but take me
there.

00:37:17.060 --> 00:37:19.860
BORIS JOHNSON: -- it is the United States.

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:27.080
Which takes people from around the world and
makes them American, and has this extraordinary

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:30.810
global brand to which everybody aspires.

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:34.110
And with the best will in the world the EU
isn't like that.

00:37:34.110 --> 00:37:39.390
The EU is composed of different proud countries,
that have their own traditions and their own

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:46.250
sense of identity, and yes I think there is
growing a kind of sense of European identity,

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:53.860
but it's nothing by comparison with the strength
of national feeling in the European continent.

00:37:53.860 --> 00:38:03.090
And so I think that the erosion of democracy
in the EU is worrying.

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:04.800
And you’re seeing some bad effects now.

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:07.530
ARTHUR BROOKS: So give us some advice here.

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:12.090
You're – we're American – you're an admirer
of the United States who – I know you love

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:14.200
this country.

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:19.610
What do you see in identity politics in America
today that reminds you a little bit too much

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:24.300
of the sub-national identities, that have
made the EU project impossible to achieve

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:28.540
its own claims due to its own internal contradictions?

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:30.460
What are the warning signs you see in America
today?

00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:37.760
BORIS JOHNSON: You know, I think it'd be impertinent
of me to comment on American politics.

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:39.930
ARTHUR BROOKS: God forbid.

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:48.120
BORIS JOHNSON: All I will say is I think that
there is a – I went to school here.

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:52.150
Other members of my family went to school
here, -- you have a thing, you pledge allegiance

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:56.180
to the flag -- just everybody sings the Star-Spangled
Banner.

00:38:56.180 --> 00:39:02.040
It's a fantastically unifying culture, and
I applaud that, and I admire that.

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:06.120
I have to say, I think it's very important.

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:11.130
As for the identity politics you know, that's
a problem everywhere, and we need to be unifying.

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:15.860
ARTHUR BROOKS: I appreciate the fact that
you admire that.

00:39:15.860 --> 00:39:19.370
I pray we never lose it.

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:20.621
BORIS HOJNSON: I (inaudible).

00:39:20.621 --> 00:39:22.260
I see no reason why you should.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:24.310
ARTHUR BROOKS: Now back to Brexit.

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:26.320
You have strong views, obviously.

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:28.560
Well known strong views on Brexit.

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:34.180
A lot of your fellow citizens in the UK disagree
with you on this.

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.320
You are a national leader.

00:39:36.320 --> 00:39:39.430
People look to you for leadership going forward
in your country.

00:39:39.430 --> 00:39:42.640
Who knows what the future might bring?

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:48.320
What are your plans for bringing America back
together as a people, across the terrible

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:50.320
fissure that is differences on Brexit?

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:54.200
BORIS JOHNSON: Well as I say, I think America
is in good shape.

00:39:54.200 --> 00:39:57.370
I'm not going to comment about it I think
it would be impertinent of me to comment about

00:39:57.370 --> 00:39:58.370
America.

00:39:58.370 --> 00:40:05.010
If I were to recover my American passport,
I might you know take a role, but that’s

00:40:05.010 --> 00:40:06.290
not going to happen.

00:40:06.290 --> 00:40:11.210
Within the UK what we need to do is get on
and deliver a great Brexit.

00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:13.840
And I have my views about how that should
be done.

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:16.430
I think it's possible to do that.

00:40:16.430 --> 00:40:26.040
I think that a vision was sketched out at
Lancaster House in January of last year, that

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:27.830
a big free trade deal and so on and so on.

00:40:27.830 --> 00:40:29.130
We need to get that done.

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:30.980
Then we need to bring people together.

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:34.900
I think people will want to come together.

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:39.750
The best thing possible to unify our country
would be, if we could have beaten the French

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:46.530
in the World Cup and that would – that really
– that would have been -- that was my plan.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:53.070
But you know we're back to the drawing board,
there.

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:56.160
We came fourth.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:59.330
We came second in the Olympics, by the way.

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:01.110
Yeah.

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:04.130
To you.

00:41:04.130 --> 00:41:05.130
It wasn't bad.

00:41:05.130 --> 00:41:10.430
ARTHUR BROOOKS: You are modest in your advice
to the United States, but I want to talk a

00:41:10.430 --> 00:41:13.700
little bit about the United States, in your
view a little bit more.

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:17.010
I want to talk about the special relationship
with the UK.

00:41:17.010 --> 00:41:19.420
We talk about it a lot.

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:25.850
It's important to us, it inspires all of us,
and I know it's important to you, and in United

00:41:25.850 --> 00:41:27.100
Kingdom as well.

00:41:27.100 --> 00:41:31.680
Tell me what does the special relationship
mean in your view?

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:34.420
Where is it and what are the perils that we
face today?

00:41:34.420 --> 00:41:38.610
BORIS JOHNSON: I banned the phrase special
relationship in the Foreign Office.

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:41.660
But because you know -- it sounds kind of
needy.

00:41:41.660 --> 00:41:46.600
I'm not sure that it's much used in Washington.

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:50.710
I know it's a phrase that was coined by, I
suppose Churchill.

00:41:50.710 --> 00:41:57.700
It was -- it's part of us trying to be more
special to you perhaps.

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:03.381
There's an asymmetry -- there's an asymmetry
in, as in so many romantic relationships there’s

00:42:03.381 --> 00:42:06.200
an asymmetry implied.

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:10.510
And that's why I reject that term.

00:42:10.510 --> 00:42:11.950
But it matters.

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:14.510
It matters hugely.

00:42:14.510 --> 00:42:22.000
We two countries do represent, I think, values
that are fantastic importance to our world.

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:25.789
Freedom, democracy, the rule of law.

00:42:25.789 --> 00:42:34.310
This stuff really, really matters, and those
values are not uncontested right now.

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:38.880
And they're not necessarily prevailing in
the way that we thought they were going to.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:42.440
And we need to work harder together.

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:47.840
And that's why the special relationship – I
use the phrase, but the UK and the U.S. working

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:49.970
together with our friends and partners is
more vital than ever before.

00:42:49.970 --> 00:42:56.580
But you know this is -- these are two countries
that are united by economics.

00:42:56.580 --> 00:43:04.770
I think we have a million people go to work
in the UK who are in the US companies, a million

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:09.980
people here in the States in British companies.

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:16.290
It's an extraordinary thing, and I guess – I'm
told you watch the Great British Bake Off.

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:19.030
Is that right?

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:20.780
There you go.

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:26.140
That the most enthusiastic response I've had
all evening, for – I don't even watch the

00:43:26.140 --> 00:43:28.341
Great British Bake Off myself I'm ashamed
to say.

00:43:28.341 --> 00:43:29.341
I'm ashamed to say.

00:43:29.341 --> 00:43:35.930
But you know, there is a huge cultural -- all
the journalists in New York seem to be Brits.

00:43:35.930 --> 00:43:36.930
It’s extraordinary.

00:43:36.930 --> 00:43:37.930
What's going on?

00:43:37.930 --> 00:43:42.860
That's an exaggeration, but you know there
are Brits everywhere here.

00:43:42.860 --> 00:43:44.240
That's a fine thing.

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:47.650
ARTHUR BROOKS: So we can't say special relationship
anymore.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:49.930
I’m trying to think of anything more modern.

00:43:49.930 --> 00:43:51.890
I'm thinking friends with benefits.

00:43:51.890 --> 00:43:54.670
BORIS JOHNSON: I don't know –

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:56.730
ARTHUR BROOKS: That means something different
here.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:57.730
Sorry.

00:43:57.730 --> 00:43:59.390
BORIS JOHNSON: Benefits.

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:05.590
You've been very influential in finding ways
of reforming benefits at the AEI, but I don’t

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:06.590
know –

00:44:06.590 --> 00:44:07.590
(Cross talk.)

00:44:07.590 --> 00:44:10.550
ARTHUR BROOKS: (Inaudible) -- that's really
the best.

00:44:10.550 --> 00:44:16.090
What do you believe, are the -- given the
fact that there is a relationship, special

00:44:16.090 --> 00:44:18.110
otherwise between the UK and the United States?

00:44:18.110 --> 00:44:21.970
What are the top foreign policy priorities,
do you think that we should be pursuing together?

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:24.200
This is something you thought a lot about
as Foreign Secretary.

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:25.200
BORIS JOHNSON: I did.

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:34.450
Well, I mean, you know, we work hand in glove
across the world, and -- get back to my main

00:44:34.450 --> 00:44:38.580
point tonight, I think trade -- free trade,
opening up the global economy, I worry about

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:42.770
-- I worry about the closing in of our approach
at the moment.

00:44:42.770 --> 00:44:48.440
Global trade is not growing in anything like
the way it should be.

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:53.950
We haven't had a big trade deal since the
end of the of the Uruguay round in 1994, I

00:44:53.950 --> 00:44:59.030
think, and which I covered myself as a journalist.

00:44:59.030 --> 00:45:03.630
We need to be doing that together.

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:08.850
We should be sorting – we should be doing
better in the Middle East.

00:45:08.850 --> 00:45:10.400
We should be working harder on Syria.

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:16.550
I got to be honest with you, I think Syria
was our policy in retrospect was not the right

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:22.280
one, but I don't want to get on that particular
rabbit hole.

00:45:22.280 --> 00:45:29.860
One thing we should do, and I this – the
biggest insight I had as Foreign Secretary

00:45:29.860 --> 00:45:37.790
is that the main problems of the world could
be addressed if we solved female under-education

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:39.150
around the world.

00:45:39.150 --> 00:45:40.150
(Applause.)

00:45:40.150 --> 00:45:47.290
If we educated women I -- when I went to sub-Saharan
Africa, or I went to South Asia I would find

00:45:47.290 --> 00:45:53.980
countries where female illiteracy was running
at 50, 60, sometimes 80 percent in parts of

00:45:53.980 --> 00:45:55.310
Africa.

00:45:55.310 --> 00:46:03.110
In Pakistan you have twice as many male kids
in secondary schools as female kids.

00:46:03.110 --> 00:46:10.400
How can that produce a healthy balanced approach
to life?

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:14.670
I just felt -- you look at radicalization,
you look at poverty.

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:17.550
All the scourges that we face.

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:24.210
They're all associated with that band of countries
where women are not treated equally – where

00:46:24.210 --> 00:46:27.380
women, and girls are not treated equally.

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:31.950
So the one thing, and it's a kind of -- do
you have a Swiss Army knife?

00:46:31.950 --> 00:46:32.950
ARTHUR BROOKS: Yes.

00:46:32.950 --> 00:46:34.880
BORIS JOHNSON: Do you know what I mean by
a Swiss Army knife?

00:46:34.880 --> 00:46:37.990
It has -- it can kind of do anything.

00:46:37.990 --> 00:46:42.920
I think female education -- 12 years of quality
education for every girl in the world would

00:46:42.920 --> 00:46:46.230
help to fix most of the problems in the world.

00:46:46.230 --> 00:46:48.320
And we should do that.

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:50.330
I really do.

00:46:50.330 --> 00:46:54.660
ARTHUR BROOKS: You have very strong views
about the future of your country, as is appropriate,

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:56.890
and a lot of people follow what you think.

00:46:56.890 --> 00:47:01.360
A lot of people admire your leadership, here
and in your own country.

00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:05.890
Obviously, your role in the leadership of
the UK could change a lot in the coming months

00:47:05.890 --> 00:47:06.890
and years.

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:08.010
You must have given it some thought.

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:09.220
I'm sure you have.

00:47:09.220 --> 00:47:14.350
Tell me the two things you'd most like to
see your government do in the coming years?

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:17.260
BORIS JOHNSON: Social mobility.

00:47:17.260 --> 00:47:18.650
Social mobility.

00:47:18.650 --> 00:47:24.610
That was you know -- you think back to the
great achievements of the Thatcher era, which

00:47:24.610 --> 00:47:27.030
I spoke of earlier.

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:33.300
It was about helping people to seize control
of their own destiny, whether it was owning

00:47:33.300 --> 00:47:38.580
shares which they hadn't thought of doing
before, or buying their own homes.

00:47:38.580 --> 00:47:46.790
Fantastically important, and since that big
change in the 1980s, social mobility is frozen

00:47:46.790 --> 00:47:48.530
again, and we need to recover that.

00:47:48.530 --> 00:47:52.040
We need to recover that momentum in the UK.

00:47:52.040 --> 00:47:56.080
And you wanted two things.

00:47:56.080 --> 00:47:58.080
That’s a big one.

00:47:58.080 --> 00:48:04.470
I think build a 24-hour hub airport, not in
my constituency.

00:48:04.470 --> 00:48:05.470
(Laughter.)

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:10.950
ARTHUR BROOKS: Number one for you, your top
agenda item for your country is greater social

00:48:10.950 --> 00:48:11.950
mobility?

00:48:11.950 --> 00:48:14.320
BORIS JOHNSON: Yes.

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:16.010
No question.

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:21.950
One of the reasons Brexit took place, or one
of the reasons people voted to leave, was

00:48:21.950 --> 00:48:25.650
because they felt they weren't getting a fair
suck of the source bottle, as they say.

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:28.170
That they were being stuck -- people are stuck
in entry-level jobs.

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:32.630
They're not progressing, and we're not focusing
on those issues.

00:48:32.630 --> 00:48:34.680
We're not helping people enough.

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:35.990
I think we discussed this earlier.

00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:41.220
They're not being made to feel needed enough.

00:48:41.220 --> 00:48:45.880
And it's a serious problem, and I think it
perhaps affects this country, too.

00:48:45.880 --> 00:48:51.080
ARTHUR BROOKS: Before we finish I have one
question, I really always wanted to ask you

00:48:51.080 --> 00:48:57.010
about your -- in your public life, you're
in the news a lot.

00:48:57.010 --> 00:49:03.550
Sometimes you see controversy, you're not
afraid to take on hottest issues of the day.

00:49:03.550 --> 00:49:08.080
Tell me the biggest mistake you've made in
your public career and what you learned from

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:09.080
it?

00:49:09.080 --> 00:49:20.440
BORIS JOHNSON: Well, my strategy is to is
to litter my career with so many decoy mistakes,

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:24.280
nobody knows which one to attack.

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:33.140
In the course of the last few minutes, I probably
said something that the British media will

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:34.920
decide is absolutely outrageous.

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:35.990
I don't know what it is.

00:49:35.990 --> 00:49:39.740
I don’t know what it was, but they will
find something, believe me.

00:49:39.740 --> 00:49:43.810
Then the thing is to keep saying them.

00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:48.620
I was very proud of a lot of the stuff I did
in London, you know, and people attacked me

00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:54.190
for the cycle superhighways for instance,
which are these beautiful lanes for the bicycles,

00:49:54.190 --> 00:49:58.380
which unfortunately block the taxis, and they
get very cross.

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:00.340
But I defend it.

00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:03.760
We save lives of cyclists, and I think it's
a beautiful thing to do for a city.

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:07.450
I'm a totally passionate cyclist, so I don’t
count that as a mistake.

00:50:07.450 --> 00:50:08.450
The biggest -- seriously –

00:50:08.450 --> 00:50:13.010
ARTHUR BROOKS: By the way, bicycles in London
are called Boris bikes for a reason.

00:50:13.010 --> 00:50:14.290
And indeed that is true.

00:50:14.290 --> 00:50:15.290
BORIS JOHNSON: They are.

00:50:15.290 --> 00:50:16.650
ARTHUR BROOKS: They are not called Boris taxis.

00:50:16.650 --> 00:50:18.540
BORIS JOHNSOPN: No, but there is.

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:19.540
We have that too.

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:20.770
There is the electric vehicles.

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:22.400
We started them anyway.

00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:24.860
It must be -- London’s going great.

00:50:24.860 --> 00:50:26.060
Come and visit.

00:50:26.060 --> 00:50:32.330
Except it was –the new guy – anyway, never
mind.

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:40.040
I did make a big -- I'll give you one mistake,
and it relates to the news today and -- how

00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:41.040
we doing?

00:50:41.040 --> 00:50:42.040
Just quickly.

00:50:42.040 --> 00:50:44.480
I’ll wrap up on this point.

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:49.220
When I became Foreign Secretary I decided
– I mean, I thought there was no objective

00:50:49.220 --> 00:50:54.280
reason why we should be quite so hostile to
Russia, okay?

00:50:54.280 --> 00:50:59.860
I thought that – yes, there were lots of
reasons to be suspicious, lots of reasons

00:50:59.860 --> 00:51:07.530
to be wary, lots of reasons to be very, very
cautious, but I thought it was possible.

00:51:07.530 --> 00:51:14.990
I made the classic, classic mistake of thinking
it was possible to have a reset with Russia.

00:51:14.990 --> 00:51:23.210
And I wanted to engage with Putin and with
Sergey Lavrov and to see if we could start

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:29.530
talking about things like Syria, areas where
we had – where we needed to engage, where

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:35.110
we needed to do things together, tackling
Islamist terrorism and so on.

00:51:35.110 --> 00:51:42.390
And then it just became clearer and clearer
to me that that was a fool's errand.

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:50.110
And finally on March the 4th, I think it was
this year, we had this outrageous event in

00:51:50.110 --> 00:51:59.350
Salisbury in Wiltshire, where two agents of
the Russian GRU were involved in a – the

00:51:59.350 --> 00:52:07.040
murder of Dawn Sturgess, and the attempted
murder of others.

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:13.640
And, you know, you see what's happening now
with these two characters produced in this

00:52:13.640 --> 00:52:22.590
satirical way by Putin on Russian TV, asked
these ludicrous patsy questions, making a

00:52:22.590 --> 00:52:27.670
mockery of the whole thing, and it really
makes my blood boil.

00:52:27.670 --> 00:52:28.670
(Applause.)

00:52:28.670 --> 00:52:38.320
And you know, I just think, for a government
that wants to be taken seriously to behave

00:52:38.320 --> 00:52:47.080
like that in 2018, we really have to treat
them with -- I mean, I hold that regime in

00:52:47.080 --> 00:52:49.070
absolute contempt.

00:52:49.070 --> 00:52:51.370
I think it's absolutely appalling.

00:52:51.370 --> 00:52:52.370
(Applause.)

00:52:52.370 --> 00:52:59.110
And you know, I'll say now, I think the you
know, -- and I say to the to those two guys

00:52:59.110 --> 00:53:03.830
who are on TV, on Russian TV – yes, they
are murderers.

00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:09.530
And if they dispute that fact, then they can
sue me in the courts.

00:53:09.530 --> 00:53:10.700
(Applause.)

00:53:10.700 --> 00:53:14.650
ARTHUR BROOKS: I think you just said news
for us.

00:53:14.650 --> 00:53:20.900
I mean, you said the biggest mistake you've
made in your public career is having trusted

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:21.900
the Russians.

00:53:21.900 --> 00:53:23.630
BORIS JOHNSON: I – it wasn't that I trusted
them.

00:53:23.630 --> 00:53:30.460
I believed I was sufficiently -- I was sufficiently
overconfident to think that I could reach

00:53:30.460 --> 00:53:32.620
out and engage and actually make a difference.

00:53:32.620 --> 00:53:34.910
And they wouldn't -- and they just haven't
changed.

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:40.790
But I'll say just one thing in conclusion,
on the special relationship – not the – I

00:53:40.790 --> 00:53:44.690
wouldn’t allow myself to use that phrase
-- on the relationship of the U.S. and the

00:53:44.690 --> 00:53:52.590
UK, what was so fantastic was, that after
the use of Novichok in Salisbury, 28 countries

00:53:52.590 --> 00:53:56.210
around the world came together, and there
are more to come by the way – came together

00:53:56.210 --> 00:54:02.350
and expelled the biggest number of Russian
spies collectively that we've ever seen.

00:54:02.350 --> 00:54:08.920
And I just want to pay a tribute to the United
States because you guys -- I'm not certain

00:54:08.920 --> 00:54:15.780
how universally popular it was, at that decision
at every level of government, but by gum,

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:17.440
you came through with the goods.

00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:18.440
(Applause.)

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:21.040
You expelled 60 Russian spies.

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:25.310
And I want to say on behalf of the British
people, we are really, really grateful for

00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:30.690
the solidarity that you showed, and you know
thank you very much.

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:36.080
And by the way, if that is a special relationship
then I'm – I'll take it.

00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:38.950
ARTHUR BROOOKS: We'll take it all day long.

00:54:38.950 --> 00:54:42.140
We're at the end of our time, Boris.

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:48.020
On behalf of my colleagues and friends, and
personally, I want to express my admiration

00:54:48.020 --> 00:54:51.340
for a lot of things that you've done, but
in particular for the attitude, the public

00:54:51.340 --> 00:54:56.110
attitude you've had of optimism toward all
the people in your society, all the people

00:54:56.110 --> 00:55:00.600
who need more mobility, all the people who
are at the periphery of society who might

00:55:00.600 --> 00:55:05.340
suffer from despair, but can hear your words
and can to hear the words of leaders, and

00:55:05.340 --> 00:55:10.510
understand that an opportunity society can
and should be available to every person in

00:55:10.510 --> 00:55:15.630
your country, and indeed that is the inspiration
behind our institution – that an opportunity

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:17.810
society should be open to everyone.

00:55:17.810 --> 00:55:23.410
For that witness, for that mission on behalf
of all of us, please join me in thanking Boris

00:55:23.410 --> 00:55:24.410
Johnson.

00:55:24.410 --> 00:55:25.410
Thank you.

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:26.950
BORIS JOHNSON: Thank you.

00:55:26.950 --> 00:55:29.270
Thank you very much.

00:55:29.270 --> 00:55:31.020
Thank you everybody.

00:55:31.020 --> 00:55:32.180
Thank you.

